@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Architecture";
}

<h1 class="mb-4">Principles used in this project</h1>

<div class="row row-cols-1 row-cols-md-2 g-4">

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-header bg-primary text-white">Abstraction</div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p class="card-text">
                    An abstractions class library holds the main <code>Book</code> class and a handful of
                    interfaces for repositories/services; there are no specifics on the database vendor.
                    Everything else in the solution depends on that project, with no other assumptions
                    other than adhering to the interfaces.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-header bg-primary text-white">SOLID (Dependency Inversion, Liskov Substitution)</div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p class="card-text">
                    I built two interchangeable storage implementations, one for ADO.NET and one for a
                    simple JSON file. These are completely independent of each other and can be used
                    interchangeably. Both are injected into the WebApp via DI.
                </p>
                <p class="card-text">
                    JSON will work out of the box, but you can edit <code>appsettings.json</code> to use
                    SQL instead (it needs a valid connection string). You will also need to run the create
                    table script if you want to use SQL (I used SQLExpress/SSMS for my testing). The JSON
                    file is stored as <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\TrustedLibrary\Json\Library.json</code>.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-header bg-primary text-white">Single Responsibility / Loose Coupling</div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p class="card-text">
                    The books collection itself is exposed as a proper REST API
                    (<code>BooksController</code>), sitting on top of the same service layer, so it's not
                    tied to the views. For a production architecture, I'd probably implement a separate
                    web service.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-header bg-primary text-white">DRY</div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p class="card-text">
                    I've employed <i>not repeating myself</i> throughout this solution, and also note the
                    decorator pattern used in the tests to ensure clean tear down.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-header bg-secondary text-white">Testing</div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p class="card-text">
                    I've written tests around the books controller, SQL repository, and JSON repository.
                    Edit the connection string in the tests project if you want those tests to succeed.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col">
        <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-header bg-secondary text-white">Out of scope</div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p class="card-text">
                    Authentication and validation were not mentioned in the requirements.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

</div>

